 {en:"The Wheel",ar:"العجلة",v:"wheel|عجلة|🎡;invent|يخترع|💡;cart|عربة|🛒;axle|محور|➖;potter|خزّاف|🏺;transport|نقل|🚚",
  c:[["When was the wheel invented?","It is believed to have been invented about five thousand years ago.","It invented the wheel by itself on Tuesday.","Wheel is inventing when."],["What was the first wheel used for?","It was probably used for making pots.","It was probably used for flying to the moon.","First is wheel used."],["How did the wheel change transport?","Heavy loads could be carried much further.","Heavy loads could be eaten much faster.","Transport is changing wheel."],["Can you imagine life without wheels?","No, there would be no cars, bikes or trains.","No, there would be no clouds, cats or soup.","Life is imagining wheels."]],
  l:"The wheel is considered one of the most important inventions in history. Interestingly, it was first used by potters to shape clay. Only later was it attached to carts to move heavy goods.",
  lq:[["How is the wheel considered?","one of the most important inventions","a modern toy","a useless idea"],["Who first used the wheel?","potters","farmers","soldiers"],["What was it attached to later?","carts","boats","houses"]],
  r:"The wheel seems so simple that it is hard to believe it was invented so late in human history. People had already been farming, building and making pottery for thousands of years before the first wheels appeared. The difficult part was not the round shape but the axle, the rod that connects two wheels. It had to be perfectly smooth and fit exactly. Once this problem was solved, wheeled carts spread quickly across the ancient world. Today, wheels are found in almost every machine, from clocks to aeroplanes.",
  rq:[["What was the difficult part of inventing the wheel?","the axle","the round shape","the colour"],["What had people been doing before wheels appeared?","farming, building and making pottery","flying","driving"],["Where are wheels found today?","in almost every machine","only in cars","only in museums"]]},
 {en:"Paper",ar:"الورق",v:"paper|ورق|📄;ink|حبر|🖋️;scroll|لفافة|📜;library|مكتبة|📚;pulp|لب الخشب|🪵;notebook|دفتر|📓",
  c:[["Where was paper first made?","It was first made in China about two thousand years ago.","It was first made on the moon by cats.","Paper is making where."],["How did paper reach the Arab world?","It is said that it came through Samarkand.","It is said that it came through the fridge.","Paper is reaching Arab."],["What was used for writing before paper?","People wrote on clay, stone and animal skins.","People wrote on clouds and bananas.","Writing is before paper."],["Why is recycling paper important?","Because fewer trees need to be cut down.","Because paper gets lonely in the bin.","Recycling is important paper."]],
  l:"Baghdad had its first paper mill more than twelve hundred years ago. Soon, paper was being used across the Islamic world. Libraries grew, and thousands of books were copied by hand.",
  lq:[["When did Baghdad get its first paper mill?","more than twelve hundred years ago","fifty years ago","last century"],["Where was paper soon being used?","across the Islamic world","only in China","nowhere"],["How were books copied?","by hand","by machines","by robots"]],
  r:"Before paper, writing materials were either heavy, like clay tablets, or expensive, like parchment made from animal skin. Paper was invented in China and was kept secret for centuries. In the eighth century, the technique reached Samarkand, and from there it spread to Baghdad, Damascus and Cairo. Arab papermakers improved the process by using linen instead of tree bark. Because paper was cheap and light, books became more common, and knowledge spread faster than ever before. Many historians say that this paper revolution helped create the golden age of Islamic science.",
  rq:[["What were writing materials like before paper?","heavy or expensive","cheap and light","made of plastic"],["What did Arab papermakers use instead of tree bark?","linen","sand","glass"],["What did the paper revolution help create?","the golden age of Islamic science","the first car","the internet"]]},
 {en:"The Compass",ar:"البوصلة",v:"compass|بوصلة|🧭;magnet|مغناطيس|🧲;needle|إبرة|🪡;navigate|يهتدي في الطريق|⛵;pole|قطب|🌐;sailors|بحّارة|🚢",
  c:[["How does a compass work?","Its needle is pulled towards the north by the Earth's magnetism.","Its needle is pulled towards the fridge by the moon.","Compass is working how."],["Who used the compass for navigation first?","It is thought that Chinese sailors used it first.","It is thought that penguins used it first.","Navigation is using who."],["Can a compass be used in a city?","Yes, but metal buildings can affect it.","Yes, but sandwiches can affect it.","City is using compass."],["Do you know which way is north right now?","Let me check the compass on my phone.","Let me check the compass in my soup.","North is knowing now."]],
  l:"A compass needle is a tiny magnet. It always points north because the Earth acts like a giant magnet. Sailors and travellers have used compasses for nearly a thousand years.",
  lq:[["What is a compass needle?","a tiny magnet","a piece of wood","a small light"],["Why does it point north?","The Earth acts like a giant magnet.","The wind pushes it.","It is painted red."],["How long have compasses been used?","nearly a thousand years","ten years","a hundred thousand years"]],
  r:"Before the compass, sailors relied on the sun, the stars and the coastline to find their way. On cloudy days or far from land, they could easily get lost. The magnetic compass changed sea travel completely. Arab navigators, such as the famous Ahmad ibn Majid, wrote detailed guides that combined the compass with knowledge of the stars and the winds. These guides were used to sail across the Indian Ocean between Arabia, India and East Africa. Today, ships use satellites, but every ship still carries a magnetic compass in case the electronics fail.",
  rq:[["What did sailors rely on before the compass?","the sun, the stars and the coastline","phones","radio"],["Who was Ahmad ibn Majid?","a famous Arab navigator","a king","a chef"],["Why do ships still carry a magnetic compass?","in case the electronics fail","for decoration","to cook"]]},
 {en:"Glasses",ar:"النظارات",v:"glasses|نظارة|👓;lens|عدسة|🔍;optician|أخصائي بصريات|🧑‍⚕️;blurry|ضبابي|🌫️;contact lens|عدسة لاصقة|👁️;sight|البصر|👀",
  c:[["Why do you wear glasses?","Because things far away look blurry.","Because my nose gets cold.","Glasses is wearing why."],["When were glasses invented?","They were invented in Italy about seven hundred years ago.","They were invented in the fridge yesterday.","Glasses is inventing when."],["Who explained how lenses work?","Ibn al-Haytham, a great Arab scientist.","Ibn Sandwich, a great Arab biscuit.","Lenses is explaining who."],["How often should you have your eyes checked?","Once a year is a good idea.","Once a century is a good idea.","Eyes is checking often."]],
  l:"Huda couldn't read the board at school. The words looked blurry. Her mother took her to an optician, who tested her eyes. Now she wears purple glasses and can see everything clearly.",
  lq:[["What couldn't Huda read?","the board at school","her book","the clock at home"],["Who tested her eyes?","an optician","a dentist","her teacher"],["What colour are her glasses?","purple","red","black"]],
  r:"About a thousand years ago, the scientist Ibn al-Haytham wrote the Book of Optics in Cairo. He was among the first to explain correctly that we see because light travels from objects into our eyes, not the other way round. He also studied how curved glass bends light. His book was translated into Latin and influenced European scientists for centuries. A few hundred years later, Italian craftsmen made the first reading glasses. Today, more than half of all adults wear glasses or contact lenses at some point in their lives.",
  rq:[["What did Ibn al-Haytham write?","the Book of Optics","a cookbook","a travel guide"],["What did he explain correctly?","Light travels from objects into our eyes.","Our eyes send out light.","We see with our ears."],["Who made the first reading glasses?","Italian craftsmen","Chinese sailors","Arab merchants"]]},
 {en:"The Printing Press",ar:"المطبعة",v:"print|يطبع|🖨️;press|مطبعة|📰;page|صفحة|📃;copy|نسخة|📑;newspaper|جريدة|🗞️;letters|حروف|🔤",
  c:[["How were books copied before printing?","They were copied by hand, one page at a time.","They were copied by camels, one page at a time.","Books is copying before."],["Who invented the printing press in Europe?","Johannes Gutenberg is usually given the credit.","Johannes Sandwich is usually given the credit.","Press is inventing who."],["How did printing change the world?","Books became cheaper and more people could read.","Books became wetter and more people could swim.","Printing is changing how."],["Do you prefer printed books or e-books?","I prefer printed books because I like turning the pages.","I prefer printed books because they taste nice.","Prefer is books printed."]],
  l:"Before the printing press, a single book could take months to copy by hand. With the press, hundreds of copies could be made in a few days. Prices fell, and more people learned to read.",
  lq:[["How long could copying one book take?","months","minutes","seconds"],["How many copies could the press make in a few days?","hundreds","two","millions"],["What happened to prices?","They fell.","They rose.","They stayed the same."]],
  r:"Printing was not invented in one place at one time. In China, people printed with carved wooden blocks more than a thousand years ago, and movable type made of clay was later developed there too. In the fifteenth century, Johannes Gutenberg built a press in Germany that used metal letters which could be arranged and reused. His system was fast and accurate. Within fifty years, millions of books had been printed in Europe. Printing in Arabic took longer to develop because of the connected letters, but today Arabic books are printed all over the world.",
  rq:[["What did people in China print with?","carved wooden blocks","computers","stones"],["What did Gutenberg's press use?","metal letters that could be reused","paint brushes","photographs"],["Why did printing in Arabic take longer?","because of the connected letters","because nobody wanted books","because there was no paper"]]},
 {en:"The Internet",ar:"الإنترنت",v:"internet|الإنترنت|🌐;website|موقع إلكتروني|🖥️;email|بريد إلكتروني|📧;password|كلمة مرور|🔐;online|متصل بالإنترنت|📶;connect|يتصل|🔗",
  c:[["How long have you been using the internet?","I've been using it since I was six.","I've been using it since I was a fish.","Internet is using long."],["Why should you keep your password secret?","So nobody else can get into your account.","So your password doesn't get cold.","Password is secret why."],["What should you do if a stranger messages you online?","Don't reply, and tell a parent straight away.","Send them your address and photos.","Stranger is messaging online."],["How is the internet useful for school?","We can find information and do research.","We can find dinosaurs in the oven.","Useful is school internet."]],
  l:"The internet connects billions of computers around the world. Messages are broken into small pieces, sent through cables and satellites, and put together again. It all happens in less than a second.",
  lq:[["What does the internet connect?","billions of computers","only phones","two cities"],["What happens to messages?","They are broken into small pieces.","They are printed.","They are lost."],["How long does it take?","less than a second","one hour","one day"]],
  r:"The internet began as a small network connecting a few universities in the United States in the late nineteen sixties. For many years, it was used mainly by scientists. In the nineteen nineties, the World Wide Web made it easy for anyone to visit websites, and the internet grew very quickly. Today, more than five billion people are online. The internet has made learning, shopping and keeping in touch easier than ever. However, it is important to use it wisely: check whether information is true, protect your personal details and take breaks from screens.",
  rq:[["How did the internet begin?","as a small network connecting universities","as a game","as a phone company"],["How many people are online today?","more than five billion","five million","five hundred"],["What is it important to do?","check information and protect your details","share your password","stay online all night"]]}
